WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:04.390
Why do scientists spend so much time
and effort studying fruit flies? Is it

2
00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:09.230
because they're all bananas? No. Flies
are actually a lot like us. They have

3
00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:14.800
hearts, brains, muscles and eyes. All
organisms evolve from a common ancestor,

4
00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:19.070
and many biological parts haven't
changed much. They are very similar in

5
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.110
every organism. Because these parts
work well and are so useful, they tend

6
00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:28.200
to stay the same. Think about a car.
There have been lots of changes in

7
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.950
design, but the wheels always stay the
same shape.

8
00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:36.430
In broad strokes, humans divide their
cells in the same way as simple cells,

9
00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:41.330
like yeast. Every organism with the
nucleus copies its DNA and separates

10
00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:46.660
the copies to two new cells, using the
same molecular machines. It's faster,

11
00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:51.130
easier and cheaper to study a yeast
cell than a human cell, so it makes

12
00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:55.590
sense to start studying cell divisions
here. It turns out that many of the

13
00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.960
basic principles that we have
discovered in yeast also apply to

14
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.050
humans, so we say that yeast is a good
model organism to study cell division.

15
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.580
Indeed, the lessons we learned in yeast
greatly accelerated our understanding

16
00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:11.950
of cell division and how it goes wrong
in diseases like cancer.

17
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.610
Over the past century, scientists have
found ideal organisms to study each

18
00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:22.320
specific scientific problem. Fruit
flies and roundworms have very similar

19
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.930
neurons to humans and are able to learn
and make complex decisions. These are

20
00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:31.810
great model organisms for neurobiology.
Fish are a great model to study early

21
00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:35.450
development. In the beginning, fish
look very similar to people.

22
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:41.160
So you see, we think like a fly, we
divide like yeast and our bodies start

23
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.950
out like fish. The underlying machinery
of life is often very similar, even in

24
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:50.650
seemingly very different organisms. But
of course, the details matter too.

